 of operations.\n\nLet me also think about one more thing: the `p0 @ G` computation. We need it if we use the quadratic formula for ||m||. But do we gain anything by needing p0@G vs p_mix@G?\n\nActually, here's another idea: we need `p_mix @ v` for the output. What if we compute `p0 @ v` and `p1 @ v` (two smaller... no, same size) and also compute the dot products for norms using those? No, the norms are in x-space not v-space.\n\nWait! I just had an idea. What about this:\n\nWe need `r = ||p1@x|| / ||p_mix@x||`.\n\nWhat if we use the identity:\n`(p@x) @ (p@x)^T = p @ (x@x^T) @ p^T = p @ G @ p^T`\n\nWe only need the diagonal of this (the norms), which we get via `(p @ G * p).sum(-1)`.\n\nBut what if we compute `p @ G` using a different method? Since G = xx^T, we have:\n`p @ G = p @ xx^T = (p @ x) @ x^T`\n\nSo `||p@x||² = ((p@x) @ x^T * p).sum(-1)`. Not helpful.\n\nWhat about using the SVD of G or something? No, that would be more expensive.\n\nLet me take a step back and think about whether there's a different mathematical formulation that's faster on CPU.\n\nActually, let me try something very practical: implement the best version I can think of, combining all the small optimizations I've identified, and run the full benchmark. The key optimizations I want to try:\n\n1. Use `addmm_` or pre-allocated output for projections (small gain)\n2. More aggressive in-place operations\n3. Avoid unnecessary intermediate tensors\n4. Compute p0@G and p1@G (instead of p1@G and p_mix@G) and use quadratic formula for ||m|| — let me check if this is actually faster by saving the p_mix computation\n   - Actually we need p_mix for p_mix@v. Unless we compute output as (1-beta)*p0@v + beta*p1@v.\n   - Let me check: is 2×(L²D) + elementwise faster than 1×(L²D) + lerp(L²)?\n   - L²D = 4.2M, L² = 65K. Two L²D matmuls = 8.4M ops vs one = 4.2M ops. \n   - The saving would be: avoiding p_mix (L² elementwise = 65K) + saving 1 L³ matmul (16.8M) but adding 1 L²D matmul (4.2M).\n   - Net saving: 