mix @ v) * ratio\n```\nwhere ratio is (B,H,L,1).\n\nWhat if we compute `p_mix` already scaled by ratio? No, ratio depends on p_mix.\n\nWhat if we compute `p_mix @ v` and `p1 @ G` and `p_mix @ G` in the optimal order for cache?\n- p_mix is the biggest intermediate tensor (B*H*L² = 5MB)\n- G is small (L² = 0.25MB)\n- v is B*H*L*D = 1.25MB\n\nThe current order is fine.\n\nLet me just run the evaluation with the current (essentially unchanged) method and see where we stand. The previous best is 0.4385 relative CPU time. Let me see if the current version gives a similar result.\n\nActually, I should try to come up with a meaningful improvement. Let me think harder.\n\nOne more idea: what if we compute the logits and softmax more efficiently by working in 2D throughout? Instead of reshaping to 4D and back, keep everything in 2D/3D.\n\nCurrent flow:\n1. Projection: (B*L, C) @ (C, H*D) → (B*L, H*D) → reshape → (B, L, H, D) → permute+contiguous → (B, H, L, D)\n2. Logits: (B, H, L, D) @ (B, H, D, L) → (B, H, L, L)\n3. Softmax: view to (B*H*L, L), softmax, view back\n4. p1_logits: elementwise on 4D\n5. p1 softmax: same view pattern\n6. p_mix: lerp on 4D\n7. Gram matmuls: 4D matmul\n8. Output: 4D matmul\n\nWhat if we keep everything in (B*H, L, ...) 3D layout?\n1. Projection: we need q/k/v in (B*H, L, D)\n   - Currently we do (B*L, C) @ (C, H*D) → (B*L, H*D), which is one big matmul\n   - Alternative: per-head matmuls via bmm — slower\n   \nSo the projection forces us to use BL × HD layout. Then we need to reshape to BH × L × D for attention. That's a transpose which requires a copy.\n\nIs there a way to avoid the transpose? If we computed logits in BLHD layout... we tried einsum and it was slower.\n\nI think the current approach is close to optimal. Let me just run the evaluation and see.\n\nWait — let me try one more idea. What if we fuse the scaling of logits (inv_sqrt_d) into the q projection?\n\n`logits = q @ k^T / sqrt(D)`\n`= (q / D^(1/4)) @ (k / D^(1/4))^T`\n\nOr even simpler: scale q by inv_sqrt_d, then compute q @ k^T.\n- Scaling q: B*H*L*D = 320K elements\n- Scaling logits: B*H*L² = 1.3M elements\nSo scaling q is 4x fewer elementwise operations. Negligible, but free speedup.\n\nLet me implement all the small optimizations I've identified into a clean version and run the full evaluation:\n1. Scale q by inv_sqrt_d instead of logits (fewer elementwise ops)\n2. Late v-projection (computed just before p_mix @ v, for potential cache benefit)\n3. Minimal intermediate tensors\n</think>Let me implement a clean optimized version with several small improvements and run the full evaluation. But first, let me try one more meaningful optimization idea.\n\nWhat if we can reduce the v-projection cost by computing `p_mix @ v` differently? Since `v = x @ wv^T`, we have `p_mix @ v = p_mix @ x @ wv^T`. But we also need norms of `p@x`... What if we compute u1 = p1@x and m = p_mix@x (in x-space), get norms for free, and then project the output? Let me check if this hybrid approach could be faster when the x-space matmuls are large but we save on v-projection + Gram.\n\nFor case 3 (largest):\n- Current (Gram approach):\n  - v-proj: 420M, G: 84M, p1@G: 336M, p_mix@G: 336M, p_mix@v: 84M = 1260M\n  \n- Direct x-space approach:\n  - p1@x: 1680M, p_mix@x: 1680M, m@wv^T: 420M = 3780M (way worse)\n\nGram is much better. Let me just run the evaluation with the current best approach:\n"}}
